% Cases found while bringing up the Rust host (2026-09-30). Each one is a place
% where a host answered differently from the others and no case said which was
% right; the answers below are what JS, PHP, Python, C++, Lisp and Go agree on.

% --- FILTER always answers a list container ------------------------------------

### name: agg.filter.scalar-source-keeping-nothing-is-not-null
--- note
SPEC 7.3: FILTER's result is a list. A scalar source is its own single child, and when the predicate drops it the answer is the empty list, which is not NULL (a host built the result as a record and returned NULL for no entries).
--- source
IS_NULL(FILTER(5, _ > 7))
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: agg.filter.record-source-keeping-nothing-is-not-null
--- source
IS_NULL(FILTER(RECORD("a", 1), _ > 7))
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: agg.filter.empty-result-skips-coalesce
--- note
`??` only replaces NULL; an empty list is a value.
--- source
COUNT(FILTER(5, FALSE) ?? LIST(1, 2))
--- expect
text "0"
===

### name: agg.filter.empty-result-has-no-scalar
--- note
The empty list has neither a scalar nor children, which is E_NO_SCALAR, not E_NULL.
--- source
FILTER(RECORD("a", 1), _ > 7) $<= "a"
--- expect
error E_NO_SCALAR at 1:1
===

% --- A quantifier binds to a whole code point -----------------------------------

### name: re.reject.nullable-loop-body.non-ascii-literal
--- note
SPEC 7.8: a loop whose body can match the empty string is refused. `é?` is an optional single code point; a structure check that steps through the pattern by bytes binds the `?` to the last byte of `é` only and wrongly sees a body that must match something.
--- source
RFIND('(?:é?)+', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:7
===

### name: re.reject.nullable-loop-body.astral-literal
--- source
RFIND('(😀?)*', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:7
===

### name: re.reject.nullable-loop-body.non-ascii-counted
--- source
RFIND('a(?:😀{0,2})+', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:7
===

### name: re.accept.non-ascii-loop-that-must-consume
--- note
The same shapes are fine when the body has to match at least one code point.
--- source
RFIND('(?:é|a)(?:é|a)*b', "ééab")
--- expect
text "1"
===
